---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import KpiDashboard from '../../components/KpiDashboard.astro';

const pages = [
  'Home',
  'Farmer support and visits',
  'Site visits',
  'Site visit follow-up',
  'Outplanting operations',
  'EOS and LOG',
  'Contracts',
  'Farmer status',
  'Project and team oversight',
  'Polygon overview',
  'Farmer detail',
];
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="program-kpi"
  facts={[
    ['Where', 'Trees for the Future, Kenya'],
    ['Tools', 'Power BI Desktop, Power Query, DAX'],
    ['Source', 'Salesforce, read directly, plus one SharePoint sheet'],
  ]}
>
  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Kenya field reporting ran on two Excel workbooks. One held a row per farmer and was refreshed by hand. The
        other took pasted form submissions and was recalculated each time.
      </p>
      <p>The aim was one model that refreshes itself and can later take in Uganda and Tanzania.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I did</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The model reads Salesforce directly through Power Query: farmers, project units, contracts, farmer status
        history, site visits, farm polygons and the two field forms, NEST for nurseries and ROOT for outplanting. One
        signing attendance sheet comes from SharePoint. Nothing is pasted in by hand. The model has 32 tables, 24
        relationships and 345 measures.
      </p>
      <p>Most of the work was deciding what to count. Four decisions changed the totals:</p>
      <ul class="list">
        <li>
          A physical visit is counted once per farmer per day. Two forms filled in on the same visit no longer make two
          visits.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tree totals and outplanting completion use each farmer's latest ROOT record. Adding up every past snapshot
          would count the same trees repeatedly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Contract signing is measured against active and reinstated farmers only, so farmers who have left the
          programme do not pull the percentage down.
        </li>
        <li>
          Planting challenges arrive from Salesforce as one multi-select text field. The model splits it, so each
          reason is counted against the visits that reported it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The Kenya report has {pages.length} pages: {pages.join(', ').toLowerCase().replace('eos and log', 'EOS and LOG')}. Every page
        prints when Salesforce was last pulled and the time of the newest record, so a stale refresh is visible. A
        Uganda report runs on the same model, and a script copies new shared pages across to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before a change is opened in Power BI, a check script reads the project files and confirms that every field a
        visual uses exists in the model.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <KpiDashboard />

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Reading the demo</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This is a web rebuild of four of those pages on generated data: farmer support and visits, outplanting
        operations, contracts and farmer status. The region and project unit filters work across all four, as the
        slicers do in the report. Pages that list individual farmers or staff are left out.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Result</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every KPI in the programme's KPI document is implemented as a measure, and the totals were reconciled against
        the live data. The report package passed Power BI's report-format validator with no errors or warnings.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pages carry their own data quality counts: site visits with no date or a date in the future, farms without
        exactly one current polygon, and EOS and LOG records with issues. Each count comes with the list of records
        behind it, so field staff can fix them at the source.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Tanzania is next. The script that copies shared pages to the Uganda report already takes a country option, so
        Tanzania needs only its own report file.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    list-style: square;
  }

  .list ::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
